// Shown without its protocol, as the room would type it.
const templateUrl = new URL("/story-map-template.xlsx", Astro.site);
const template = `${templateUrl.host}${templateUrl.pathname}`;
---

<TitleSlide
  kicker="CS 461, fall week 2: Workshop 1"
  title="User Story Mapping"
  subtitle="A backbone, a release line, and a walking skeleton for your project"
  byline="CS 461: the instruction team"
>
  <Notes time="0:20" cue="Teams sit together by team number; one laptop per team opens the template.">
    <p>
      By the end of class each team has a backbone, a walking-skeleton row,
      and its assumptions flagged. The next slide is the plan for the hour.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</TitleSlide>

<Slide kicker="Agenda" title="Today, in four parts">
  <ol class="agenda">
    <li><span class="minute">Minute 1</span>Story maps in three minutes</li>
    <li><span class="minute">Minute 4</span>Three examples: ephira, LumiLab, TaxonBodyMassML</li>
    <li><span class="minute">Minute 11</span>The same proposal, mapped by AI</li>
    <li>
      <span class="minute">Minute 15</span>Your turn: map in teams, a clinic at
      minute 30, the wrap at minute 45
    </li>
  </ol>
  <Notes time="0:40" cue="Point at minute 15: that is when the laptops open.">
    <p>
      Fifteen minutes of talk, thirty of mapping, five of wrap. The three
      examples use three different backbones, so every team should see one
      that looks like its own project. The AI part maps one of those
      examples again, with a tool, so the room can compare the two maps row
      by row.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Part 1: story maps in three minutes" title="What a story map is made of">
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Backbone:</strong> the big activities across the top, in the
      order they happen
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>Stories:</strong> the smaller steps under each activity, higher
      when more necessary
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>Release line:</strong> a cut across the map; everything above it
      ships first
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>Walking skeleton:</strong> the first slice, a story under every
      activity, so the whole thing runs end to end
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="caption">
    Jeff Patton, The New User Story Backlog is a Map:{" "}
    <a href={patton.href}>{patton.text}</a>
  </p>
  <Notes time="1:30" cue="Press the arrow for each line; ask the room which line is the hardest to draw.">
    <p>
      Jeff Patton's article, linked on the slide, is where the map comes
      from. He does not prioritize the backbone: it is the set of things the
      system has to do at all. He prioritizes the stories hanging under it,
      moving them up or down, and lays tape across the map to make one lane
      per release.
    </p>
    <p>
      The stories placed highest form the smallest system that works end to
      end. Patton credits the term walking skeleton to Alistair Cockburn; use
      Patton's description here and don't paraphrase Cockburn's. His
      complaint about a flat backlog is that a list in build order can't
      tell anyone what the system does, or show what you missed; a map can,
      because you can walk it left to right with a user.
    </p>
    <p>
      One convention for today: a story nobody has checked with the project
      partner starts with a question mark, and the template turns it amber.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Part 1: story maps in three minutes" title="Choose the backbone where your hardest unknowns live">
  <div class="backbones">
    <div class="type">
      <h3>User journey</h3>
      <p>What a user does, in order</p>
      <p class="skeleton">Skeleton: one user gets through every activity once</p>
    </div>
    <div class="type fragment">
      <h3>Pipeline stages</h3>
      <p>What happens to one input on its way to an output</p>
      <p class="skeleton">Skeleton: one real input reaches an output, stubs allowed</p>
    </div>
    <div class="type fragment">
      <h3>Research chain</h3>
      <p>Question, data, baseline, method, evaluation, result</p>
      <p class="skeleton">Skeleton: the baseline runs end to end and produces one number</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="fragment rule-line">
    Same rows, release line, <code>?</code> flags, and partner questions for all
    three. Write one line saying why you chose yours.
  </p>
  <Notes time="1:30" cue="Ask for hands: who is not sure their project has a user?">
    <p>
      Why it matters: the walking skeleton should exercise the riskiest part
      of the project first, so the backbone has to run through it. In a
      recent year, about half the project listings had no clean user journey
      to map (the instruction team's own count), so a good part of the room
      needs this slide.
    </p>
    <p>
      A game is a user journey with the player as the user: launch, learn the
      controls, play a level, fail and retry, finish. A library or an API is
      a user journey with the adopting developer as the user: find it,
      install it, get a first call working, integrate it, upgrade. Pick the
      backbone where the hardest unknowns are: if the scary part is whether
      a model beats a baseline, map the research chain, even if there is a
      web front end too.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<StatementSlide kicker="Part 2 of 4: minute 4" source="ephira, a user journey; LumiLab, a pipeline; TaxonBodyMassML, a research chain">
  Three examples
  <Notes time="0:10" cue="One backbone type each, in the order of the last slide.">
    <p>
      Each example opens with the project, then its map, then what the team
      did in its year.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</StatementSlide>

<Slide kicker="Example 1: ephira, a user journey" title="A private period tracker, already in both app stores">
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The app:</strong> a menstrual cycle tracker for iOS and Android
      that keeps every record on the phone
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>Where it started:</strong> a previous capstone team built it and
      published it in both app stores
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>The advertised project:</strong> enhance it, with predictions,
      reminders, encrypted backups, easier data entry, tests, and a store
      update
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="caption">
    Repository: <a href={ephira.repo.href}>{ephira.repo.text}</a><br />
    Project page: <a href={ephira.page.href}>{ephira.page.text}</a>
  </p>
  <Notes time="0:40" cue="Ask: what should the map's first row hold, for a project like this?">
    <p>
      ephira is open source under the MIT license; its project page calls it
      privacy-focused and local-first, and says the project "involves
      enhancing Ephira's functionality". The app was at version 1.1.0 in
      both stores when the advertised team started.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the map's first row holds what the repository already does, read
      from the code before anyone plans anything; the plans go in the rows
      under it.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide title="ephira: start the map from what already ships" wide>
  <StoryMap {...ephiraMap} />
  <p class="caption caption-tight">
    Also on the <a href={ephira.page.href}>project page</a>: beta tests, docs, a
    store update; start designing and building pregnancy mode
  </p>
  <Notes time="1:10" cue="Read the inherited skeleton left to right, then point at the empty reminders cell.">
    <p>
      The inherited skeleton is what the repository already did the day the
      team started: a password or biometric lock, a full day log, the
      calendar and its filter, export as CSV, JSON, or PDF, and a first
      prediction that added 28 days to the last period but did not work, so
      the card says so. There were no
      reminders, so the inherited skeleton has a gap, and the advertised
      notifications fill it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Release 1 is the advertised deliverables. Prediction from history is
      stated plainly, so it's known. The other four are questions for the
      partner. Reminders come in the two kinds the proposal names: alerts
      about upcoming cycle events, and data-entry reminders. The alerts get
      the question mark because the proposal asks for push notifications,
      and a server-sent alert about a predicted period means the prediction
      leaves the phone; a daily reminder to log needs no data, so it's
      known. Encrypted
      export and import: the proposal says encrypted, but not how the key is
      made or kept, and nobody had designed it. The two cards about data
      entry and charts are the team's reading of "UI/UX improvements".
    </p>
    <p>
      The stretch goal gets its own row because the proposal labels it one:
      opt-in anonymized data collection, under Take the data out because
      it's data leaving the phone. Cloud sync is the team's own assumption:
      the proposal never mentions it, and it's below the line because
      syncing means an account and a sign-in. Beta testing, docs, the store
      update, and pregnancy mode are advertised but aren't activities on
      this backbone, so they sit in the caption.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<TwoColumnSlide kicker="Example 1: ephira, what happened" title="What the team built in its year" divider>
  <div slot="left">
    <h3>Added</h3>
    <ul class="reveal-list">
      <li>Faster logging: a chip grid and one-tap birth control</li>
      <li>A Cycle tab: predictions from average cycle length, phases, and tips</li>
      <li>Alerts before a predicted period, scheduled on the phone</li>
      <li>A guided walkthrough, and the first pregnancy mode tabs</li>
      <li>Unit tests, and app version 1.1.0 to 1.3.0</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div slot="right">
    <h3>Not built</h3>
    <ul class="reveal-list">
      <li>Encrypted export and import: the export is still the inherited one, with no import</li>
      <li>Reminders to log a day: a message template, never scheduled</li>
      <li>Integration and end-to-end tests: unit tests only</li>
      <li>Cloud sync and the stretch goal: not attempted</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="caption">
      Repository history: <a href={ephira.repo.href}>{ephira.repo.text}</a>
    </p>
  </div>
  <Notes time="0:45" cue="Ask: which amber card would you have checked with the partner first?">
    <p>
      From the repository history over the team's three terms. Two Release
      1 cards didn't land: the encrypted export and import, an amber card,
      and the log reminders, a known one.
    </p>
    <p>
      The proposal asked for push notifications; the team scheduled the
      period alerts on the phone instead, which settles the amber card in
      favor of the product's promise that nothing leaves the device. The template for a reminder to log more data exists
      in the code, but nothing ever schedules it. The tests are unit tests;
      the advertised integration and end-to-end tests aren't in the
      repository. The commits after the team's year are the maintainer's;
      don't credit them to the team.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</TwoColumnSlide>

<Slide kicker="Example 2: LumiLab, a pipeline" title="Measuring glare from ordinary photos">
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Glare:</strong> light bright enough to make seeing
      uncomfortable. Measuring it takes a luminance camera, which few can
      afford
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>What goes in:</strong> the same scene photographed several
      times, dark to bright, with an ordinary camera, plus that camera's
      calibration files
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>What comes out:</strong> a luminance map, an image where every
      pixel holds how bright that spot was, in measured units
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>Who uses it:</strong> lighting designers and daylighting
      researchers
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="caption">
    Repository: <a href={lumiLab.repo.href}>{lumiLab.repo.text}</a><br />
    Project page: <a href={lumiLab.page.href}>{lumiLab.page.text}</a>
  </p>
  <Notes time="0:45" cue="Ask: is there a user clicking through anything here?">
    <p>
      Luminance is how bright a surface looks, measured in candela per
      square meter. The technique is high dynamic range imaging: several
      exposures of one scene, merged and corrected step by step, give the
      same measurement as the expensive camera. The steps come from the
      tutorial the repository's README cites, Pierson, Cauwerts, Bodart, and
      Wienold, "Tutorial: Luminance Maps for Daylighting Studies from High
      Dynamic Range Photography" (<a
        href="https://doi.org/10.1080/15502724.2019.1684319"
        >doi.org/10.1080/15502724.2019.1684319</a
      >), and doing them by hand takes command-line tools and some
      programming.
    </p>
    <p>
      A previous capstone team had built a first desktop app for it. The
      advertised project was to improve that app, "we expect refactoring",
      with installers for Windows and macOS and documentation. The user
      clicks one button; the hard part is what happens to the photos after
      that, so the backbone on the next slide follows the photos.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Example 2: LumiLab, the map" title="One set of photos on its way to a luminance map" wide>
  <StoryMap
    backbone={[
      "Pick the photos",
      "Merge into one image",
      "Crop and resize",
      "Correct the lens",
      "Calibrate the brightness",
      "Save the map",
    ]}
    releases={[
      {
        name: "Walking skeleton",
        stories: [
          "Choose photos in the app",
          "Merge with one camera's settings",
          "Fixed numbers for one lens",
          "Fisheye and dark-edge fixes",
          "Pass it through unchanged",
          "Write one .hdr file",
        ],
      },
      {
        name: "Release 1",
        stories: [
          undefined,
          "Load any camera's response file",
          "Type the numbers in the app",
          "Load the lens calibration files",
          ["Correct for a lens filter", "? Scale to a meter reading"],
          "Record the settings in the file",
        ],
      },
    ]}
  />
  <p class="caption">
    Inferred from the <a href={lumiLab.page.href}>project page</a> and the{" "}
    <a href={lumiLab.repo.href}>repository</a>, not the team's own map.
  </p>
  <Notes time="0:45" cue="Point at Pass it through unchanged: a stub is still a story.">
    <p>
      The columns are the steps of the published technique, in order.
      Merging also resets the exposure, so the image starts from a known
      brightness. Correcting the lens means two fixes: undoing the fisheye's
      distortion, and brightening the edges a lens darkens.
    </p>
    <p>
      The walking skeleton runs one real set of photos through every column
      with the settings fixed for one camera and lens, and lets the
      calibration column pass the image through untouched. The amber card is
      the open question: which reference reading the scale comes from is
      the partner's call, not the team's.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Example 2: LumiLab, the walking skeleton" title="The whole path by week 9, then the rest">
  <ol>
    <li>Fall week 6: the app runs its first pipeline command</li>
    <li class="fragment">Week 7: merge, crop, and resize each work, with fixed settings</li>
    <li class="fragment">Week 8: the two lens fixes</li>
    <li class="fragment">
      Week 9: one button sends the chosen photos through six stages; every
      other setting is still fixed in the code
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      Week 10: the filter, calibration, and file-header stages, and a form
      for the settings
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p class="caption">
    Repository history: <a href={lumiLab.repo.href}>{lumiLab.repo.text}</a>
  </p>
  <Notes time="0:45" cue="Ask: which rung would your project be on at the end of fall?">
    <p>
      From the commit history of the team's fall term, which rewrote the
      app with a Rust back end. The week 9 commit wires the app's Generate
      button to the whole pipeline: the user's own photos go in, and the
      camera file, the crop and size numbers, and the folders are written
      into the code. The history doesn't say whether that first run produced
      a correct map; it does show every stage chained, in order.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is a walking skeleton: thin, hard-coded, and end to end. The
      calibration stages and the form for the settings came after, on a path
      that already ran.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Example 3: TaxonBodyMassML, a research chain" title="Estimating a species' weight from its name">
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The project:</strong> a model that estimates how much a species
      weighs from its scientific name and its relatives
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>Who uses the result:</strong> ecologists, who need the weight of
      species nobody has weighed
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>What goes in:</strong> one species name, or a list of them
    </li>
    <li class="fragment">
      <strong>What comes out:</strong> a weight estimate, where it came from,
      and how sure the model is
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="caption">
    Repository: <a href={taxon.repo.href}>{taxon.repo.text}</a><br />
    Project page: <a href={taxon.page.href}>{taxon.page.text}</a>
  </p>
  <Notes time="0:40" cue="Ask: what would tell this team its model works?">
    <p>
      Ecologists treat body mass as a master trait: it predicts metabolism,
      lifespan, and who eats whom. Thousands of species have been weighed,
      a small share of all described species, so the project predicts the
      rest from the scientific name and the species' place in the tree of
      life. The page asks for an estimate with a provenance flag and an
      uncertainty descriptor, and a web page for single and batch queries.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the map, the baseline is the simplest guess worth beating, the
      method is the model, and the evaluation is how wrong it is on species
      kept aside.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

